1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,496
This is STAT 331, video 16, part a. The topic is inference for one coefficient, beta j, in multiple linear regression.

2
00:00:10,946 --> 00:00:17,154
This part prepares you for two question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:17,604 --> 00:00:32,217
Type A: find beta hat and the standard error of beta j hat from X transpose X inverse, X transpose y, and the sum of squared residuals. Lecture 7 and its practice question 2 do this.

4
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:43,334
Type B: test the null hypothesis beta j equals theta 0. Lecture 8 gives the test. Tutorial 3, question 1 f, uses it.

5
00:00:43,784 --> 00:00:52,659
Part b covers three more types: confidence intervals, linear combinations of the betas, and the proofs of practice question 3.

6
00:00:53,109 --> 00:00:59,743
Section 1 is a recap. We have n observations. The response vector y has n rows.

7
00:01:00,193 --> 00:01:09,068
The design matrix X has one row for each observation. Its first column is all ones. This column gives the intercept.

8
00:01:09,518 --> 00:01:17,987
Then X has one column for each covariate, x 1 to x p. So X has n rows and p plus 1 columns.

9
00:01:18,437 --> 00:01:30,299
The vector beta has p plus 1 entries: beta 0 to beta p. The error vector epsilon has n entries. So y equals X beta plus epsilon.

10
00:01:30,749 --> 00:01:44,807
The errors are multivariate normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times I. This is the same as: y is multivariate normal with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I.

11
00:01:45,257 --> 00:01:52,553
The least squares estimator is beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

12
00:01:53,003 --> 00:02:02,966
Beta hat is normal. Its mean is beta, so it is unbiased. Its variance matrix is sigma squared times X transpose X inverse.

13
00:02:03,416 --> 00:02:20,739
We call the matrix X transpose X inverse V. Its rows and columns are numbered 0 to p, like the coefficients. The diagonal entries give the variances: the variance of beta j hat is sigma squared times V j j.

14
00:02:21,189 --> 00:02:31,343
So one coefficient alone is normal too. For example, beta 2 hat is normal with mean beta 2 and variance sigma squared V 2 2.

15
00:02:31,793 --> 00:02:42,353
From Lecture 7, we also know that the residual vector e is normal, and that beta hat and e are independent. We need this independence soon.

16
00:02:42,803 --> 00:02:57,886
Section 2: the t result. First, why do we estimate sigma squared with e transpose e over n minus p plus 1, and not with the MLE, which divides by n? Line 1 is the definition.

17
00:02:58,336 --> 00:03:05,248
Take the expectation of sigma hat squared. Line 2 puts the definition inside the expectation.

18
00:03:05,698 --> 00:03:31,426
Multiply and divide by sigma squared. The constant, sigma squared over n minus p plus 1, comes out of the expectation. Inside is V, e transpose e over sigma squared. Last class showed that V is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The picture shows this density for the satisfaction data, with 42 degrees of freedom.

19
00:03:31,876 --> 00:03:43,588
The mean of a chi squared variable with nu degrees of freedom is nu. So the expectation in the frame is n minus p plus 1. The purple line marks it at 42.

20
00:03:44,038 --> 00:04:02,747
The two factors n minus p plus 1 cancel. So the expectation of sigma hat squared is sigma squared. Sigma hat squared is unbiased. The MLE divides by n, so it is too small on average. Type A uses sigma hat squared in step 3.

21
00:04:03,197 --> 00:04:12,264
Start from the distribution of beta hat. It is normal, with mean beta and variance sigma squared times X transpose X inverse.

22
00:04:12,714 --> 00:04:25,791
Take entry j. Beta j hat is normal, with mean beta j and variance sigma squared V j j. V j j is diagonal entry j of X transpose X inverse.

23
00:04:26,241 --> 00:04:38,529
Subtract the mean, and divide by the standard deviation, sigma root V j j. The result is standard normal. The gray dashed curve is this N 0 1 density.

24
00:04:38,979 --> 00:04:56,771
But sigma is unknown. Replace it with sigma hat, in orange. The result has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The red curve is a t density with 3 degrees of freedom. Its tails are heavier than the normal tails.

25
00:04:57,221 --> 00:05:13,797
The satisfaction data have 42 degrees of freedom. The blue t curve with 42 degrees of freedom is almost the normal curve. So the price for an unknown sigma is heavier tails, and with many degrees of freedom the price is small.

26
00:05:14,247 --> 00:05:27,410
Here is why the result is a t distribution. Line 1: Z is beta j hat minus beta j, over sigma root V j j. Z is standard normal, and it uses beta hat.

27
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:40,745
Line 2: W is e transpose e over sigma squared. Lecture 7 showed that W is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. W uses e.

28
00:05:41,195 --> 00:05:46,166
Beta hat and e are independent. So Z and W are independent.

29
00:05:46,616 --> 00:05:57,731
A standard normal, divided by the square root of an independent chi squared over its degrees of freedom, has a t distribution. That is the definition of t.

30
00:05:58,181 --> 00:06:15,290
Simplify the frame in line 4. W over n minus p plus 1 is e transpose e over n minus p plus 1, over sigma squared. That is sigma hat squared over sigma squared. Its square root is sigma hat over sigma.

31
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:28,625
Put Z and line 5 into T. T is Z times sigma over sigma hat. Sigma cancels. T is beta j hat minus beta j, over sigma hat root V j j.

32
00:06:29,075 --> 00:06:43,454
Sigma hat root V j j is the standard error of beta j hat. It uses only numbers from the data. So T gives tests and intervals for beta j. Types A, B and C use this result.

33
00:06:43,904 --> 00:06:56,533
This result is the same as in simple linear regression. There, the standard error was sigma hat over root S x x, and the t distribution had n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

34
00:06:56,983 --> 00:07:09,293
In multiple regression, only two things change. The standard error is the square root of sigma hat squared V j j. And the degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1.

35
00:07:09,743 --> 00:07:21,433
With p equal to 1, n minus p plus 1 is n minus 2. So simple regression is a special case. The test and the interval have the same form as before.

36
00:07:21,883 --> 00:07:34,811
Section 3. Question type A asks for beta hat and the standard errors. Lecture 7 computes them for the satisfaction data. Practice question 2 gives only parts of the matrices.

37
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:44,755
Step 1 of method card A: fill in X transpose X inverse. It is symmetric, so entry i j equals entry j i.

38
00:07:45,205 --> 00:07:59,029
Step 2: beta hat is X transpose X inverse times X transpose y. Row j of the matrix, times X transpose y, gives beta j hat. The green frame shows row 1.

39
00:07:59,479 --> 00:08:10,892
Step 3: the degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1. Sigma hat squared is the sum of squared residuals, S S E, over the degrees of freedom.

40
00:08:11,342 --> 00:08:25,187
Step 4: V j j is diagonal entry j of X transpose X inverse. Rows and columns count from 0. So for beta 1, take the entry in row 1 and column 1.

41
00:08:25,637 --> 00:08:33,317
Step 5: the standard error of beta j hat is the square root of sigma hat squared times V j j.

42
00:08:33,767 --> 00:08:49,853
A worked problem of type A. Pause the video and try it. 46 hospital patients give a satisfaction score. The covariates are age, severity of illness, and a stress score. The response vector y has 46 entries.

43
00:08:50,303 --> 00:08:54,185
To build X, start with a column of ones for the intercept.

44
00:08:54,635 --> 00:09:04,641
Then add one column for each covariate: age, severity, and stress. X has 46 rows and 4 columns. So p equals 3.

45
00:09:05,091 --> 00:09:29,880
Step 2 of card A: beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y. Beta 0 hat is 158.49. Beta 1 hat, for age, is minus 1.142. Beta 2 hat, for severity, is minus 0.442. Beta 3 hat, for stress, is minus 13.470.

46
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:41,594
Now the standard errors, with card A. Step 3: the degrees of freedom. n is 46, and p plus 1 is 4. So there are 42 degrees of freedom.

47
00:09:42,044 --> 00:09:55,505
The residuals give S S E, e transpose e, equal to 4248.84. Divide by 42. Sigma hat squared is 101.16.

48
00:09:55,955 --> 00:10:09,097
Step 4: take the diagonal of X transpose X inverse. There is one entry for each coefficient. For stress, V 3 3 is 0.4983.

49
00:10:09,547 --> 00:10:26,251
Step 5: multiply each entry by sigma hat squared. For stress, 101.16 times 0.4983 is 50.41. This is the estimated variance of beta 3 hat.

50
00:10:26,701 --> 00:10:44,365
Take the square root. For stress, the square root of 50.41 is 7.10. The table gives all four standard errors: 18.13, 0.2148, 0.4920, and 7.10.

51
00:10:44,815 --> 00:10:57,316
Sigma hat is the square root of 101.16, which is 10.06. So each standard error is sigma hat times the square root of V j j.

52
00:10:57,766 --> 00:11:12,038
Practice question 2 of Lecture 7. Pause the video and try it. The sample size is 50. Three entries in the lower corner of X transpose X inverse are lost. Can we still find beta hat?

53
00:11:12,488 --> 00:11:31,475
Yes. Step 1 of card A: X transpose X is symmetric, so its inverse is symmetric too. Each lost entry equals its mirror entry above the diagonal. So the lost entries are 0.25, 0.25, and minus 0.25.

54
00:11:31,925 --> 00:11:38,026
Step 2: beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

55
00:11:38,476 --> 00:11:50,913
Row 0: 1 times 2, plus 0.25 times 1.2, plus 0.25 times minus 3. Beta 0 hat is 1.55.

56
00:11:51,363 --> 00:12:04,825
Row 1: 0.25 times 2, plus 0.5 times 1.2, plus minus 0.25 times minus 3. Beta 1 hat is 1.85.

57
00:12:05,275 --> 00:12:17,627
Row 2: 0.25 times 2, plus minus 0.25 times 1.2, plus 2 times minus 3. Beta 2 hat is minus 5.8.

58
00:12:18,077 --> 00:12:27,314
Part b. The sum of squared residuals is 2.5. Find the standard error of beta 1 hat. Pause the video and try it.

59
00:12:27,764 --> 00:12:38,217
Step 3 of card A. The model has 3 coefficients, so p plus 1 is 3. The degrees of freedom are 50 minus 3, which is 47.

60
00:12:38,667 --> 00:12:45,707
Sigma hat squared is 2.5 over 47. That is 0.05319.

61
00:12:46,157 --> 00:12:56,845
Step 4: beta 1 is in row 1, because rows count from 0. So we need V 1 1. In the completed matrix of part a, it is 0.5.

62
00:12:57,295 --> 00:13:14,234
Step 5: the standard error is the square root of 0.05319 times 0.5. That is the square root of 0.02660, which is 0.1631.

63
00:13:14,684 --> 00:13:31,345
Section 4: tests. Question type B tests the null hypothesis beta j equals theta 0, against the alternative beta j is not equal to theta 0. Theta 0 is a fixed number. Often it is 0.

64
00:13:31,795 --> 00:13:40,734
If beta j hat is much larger than theta 0, then beta j hat minus theta 0 is a large positive number.

65
00:13:41,184 --> 00:13:47,819
If beta j hat is much smaller than theta 0, the difference is a large negative number.

66
00:13:48,269 --> 00:13:59,682
For a two-sided test, both cases are evidence against the null hypothesis. So the evidence is the absolute difference between beta j hat and theta 0.

67
00:14:00,132 --> 00:14:15,279
But how large is large? We measure the difference in units of its standard error. This gives the statistic T. Under the null hypothesis, T has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

68
00:14:15,729 --> 00:14:32,262
These two panels have the same difference between beta j hat and theta 0. Only the standard error is different. On the left, the standard error is small. The purple curve is the distribution of beta j hat if the null is true.

69
00:14:32,712 --> 00:14:47,261
Beta j hat is far out in the tail. The absolute t is 3.2. This result is surprising if the true beta j is theta 0. So a large absolute T is evidence against the null.

70
00:14:47,711 --> 00:14:52,661
On the right, the standard error is large. The null distribution is wide.

71
00:14:53,111 --> 00:15:08,449
Now the same beta j hat is near the center. The absolute t is only 1. When the standard error is large, there is much uncertainty, and nothing surprises us. So we compare t obs with the null distribution.

72
00:15:08,899 --> 00:15:23,470
The p-value is the probability, under the null, of a test statistic as extreme as the one we observed, or more extreme. Under the null, T has a t distribution with 42 degrees of freedom.

73
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:41,200
Example: test beta 3 equals 0, for stress. t obs is beta 3 hat over its standard error: minus 13.4702 over 7.0997, which is minus 1.897.

74
00:15:41,650 --> 00:15:49,373
Values at least this far below 0 are in the left tail. Its area is 0.03234.

75
00:15:49,823 --> 00:15:59,977
Values at least this far above 0 are just as extreme. By symmetry, the right tail has the same area, 0.03234.

76
00:16:00,427 --> 00:16:21,355
Add the two tails. The p-value is 2 times 0.03234, which is 0.06468, or about 0.0647. In general, the p-value is 2 times the probability that T is larger than the absolute value of t obs.

77
00:16:21,805 --> 00:16:40,429
If the null is true, small p-values are rare. Use alpha equal to 0.05. The red tails each have area alpha over 2. The cut point is t 0.975 with 42 degrees of freedom, which is 2.018.

78
00:16:40,879 --> 00:16:55,898
By convention, we reject the null if the p-value is less than alpha. This is the same as: the absolute value of t obs is larger than the cut point. If the p-value is at least alpha, we fail to reject.

79
00:16:56,348 --> 00:17:05,372
For age, t obs is minus 5.31. It is in the red region. Reject the null that beta 1 equals 0.

80
00:17:05,822 --> 00:17:25,577
For stress, t obs is minus 1.90. It is not in the red region, and the p-value 0.0647 is larger than 0.05. We fail to reject. This does not prove that beta 3 is 0. We only lack evidence.

81
00:17:26,027 --> 00:17:45,035
What happens if the null hypothesis is really true? Here is our own simulation. We made 2000 data sets from the model with beta 3 equal to 0. For each data set, we fit the model and calculated t obs for beta 3. The bars show the histogram.

82
00:17:45,485 --> 00:17:52,887
The histogram follows the t density with 42 degrees of freedom. This checks the result of this lecture.

83
00:17:53,337 --> 00:18:12,687
The red dashed lines are the two cut points of the decision rule. 80 of 2000 t values are outside. That is close to 5 percent. So if the null is true, we reject alpha times 100 percent of the time by chance alone. This is the type 1 error rate.

84
00:18:13,137 --> 00:18:20,795
Here is the method for question type B, in five steps. The picture shows each step on the null distribution.

85
00:18:21,245 --> 00:18:33,853
Step 1 of method card B: write the null and the alternative hypotheses, and the degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1. Under the null, T has this t distribution.

86
00:18:34,303 --> 00:18:41,471
Step 2: t obs is beta j hat minus theta 0, over the standard error of beta j hat.

87
00:18:41,921 --> 00:18:50,391
Step 3: the p-value is 2 times the probability that T is larger than the absolute t obs. It is the red area.

88
00:18:50,841 --> 00:19:05,347
Step 4: reject the null if the p-value is less than alpha. This is the same as: the absolute t obs is larger than the cut point t 1 minus alpha over 2. Otherwise, fail to reject.

89
00:19:05,797 --> 00:19:12,752
Step 5: conclude in words. The test is about beta j, with the other covariates in the model.

90
00:19:13,202 --> 00:19:35,773
A worked problem of type B. Pause the video and try it. Lecture 7 tests beta j equals 0 for each coefficient of the satisfaction model. Step 1 of card B: theta 0 is 0, and the degrees of freedom are 46 minus 4, which is 42. The table gives the estimates and the standard errors.

91
00:19:36,223 --> 00:19:51,796
Step 2: t obs is the estimate over its standard error. For age, minus 1.1416 over 0.2148 is minus 5.315. The other rows work the same way.

92
00:19:52,246 --> 00:20:04,171
Step 3: the p-value is 2 times the probability that T is larger than the absolute t obs. For age, it is 3.81 times 10 to the minus 6.

93
00:20:04,621 --> 00:20:24,013
Step 4: compare with alpha equals 0.05. The intercept and age have p-values below 0.05. Their t obs values are outside plus or minus 2.018. Reject. For severity and stress, fail to reject.

94
00:20:24,463 --> 00:20:45,583
Step 5: conclude in words. With severity and stress in the model, age is related to mean satisfaction. For stress, the p-value 0.0647 is not less than 0.05, so we fail to reject. This does not prove that beta 3 is 0.

95
00:20:46,033 --> 00:21:10,993
A tutorial question of type B. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 f, uses the R output of a salary model with 6 coefficients. The posted solution shows that the question is the test of beta 4 equals 0. Beta 4 is the coefficient of size f 2 times experience. Video 20 explains these terms.

96
00:21:11,443 --> 00:21:25,950
Step 1 of card B: the null is beta 4 equals 0, the alternative is beta 4 is not 0. The output gives 319 degrees of freedom: 325 minus 6.

97
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:42,592
Step 2: t obs is minus 186.99 minus 0, over the standard error 147.33. That is minus 1.269. The output prints the same t value.

98
00:21:43,042 --> 00:21:58,082
Step 3: the p-value is 2 times the probability that T, with 319 degrees of freedom, is larger than 1.269. It is 0.2053, the red area.

99
00:21:58,532 --> 00:22:13,316
Step 4: 0.2053 is not less than 0.05. So we fail to reject the null. t obs is also inside plus or minus 1.967.

100
00:22:13,766 --> 00:22:27,270
Step 5: conclude. At the 0.05 level, there is no evidence that the relationship between salary and experience differs between districts of size 1 and size 2.

101
00:22:27,720 --> 00:22:51,357
To recap part a. Beta j hat minus beta j, over sigma hat root V j j, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Card A finds beta hat and the standard errors: fill the symmetric matrix, multiply by X transpose y, find sigma hat squared, take V j j, and take the square root.

102
00:22:51,807 --> 00:23:09,962
Card B tests beta j equals theta 0: write the hypotheses and the degrees of freedom, find t obs and the p-value, decide, and conclude in words. Part b continues with confidence intervals, linear combinations, and the practice proofs.
